itial thread, storing its identifier, and then attempting to reattach to the exact same thread during reload. This methodical process ensures thread continuity and validates the implementation's adherence to the specified requirements.\n\nHowever, the `--resume` launch mode reveals a potential edge case. When no thread is explicitly selected, the system defaults to a null state, effectively treating a resumed session with no selection as equivalent to a fresh launch. This subtle behavior might introduce unexpected thread management complications.\n\nThe current implementation risks creating unintended new threads during reload scenarios, potentially diverging from the expected thread reattachment strategy outlined in the application contract.\n\nTwo distinct resolution paths emerge: `resolveReloadFor` relies on a precise thread ID from PI's durable entries, while `resolveForLaunch` defaults to creating a new thread when no explicit thread reference exists. This design choice could lead to thread state inconsistencies.\n\nThe nuanced difference suggests a deeper architectural consideration around thread preservation and session continuity during reload mechanisms.\n\nThe core dispute centers on whether creating a fresh thread during an unspecificated reload violates the original specification. The epic's language implies maintaining thread continuity, yet the implementation allows thread generation when no explicit thread identifier is present.\n\nCritically, this isn't an implementation defect, but a deliberate design decision with nuanced implications for session management and thread persistence.\n\nThe potential edge case involves multiple reload scenarios with no pre-existing thread identifier, which might inadvertently create separate threads instead of maintaining a single session context.\n\nCurrent test coverage confirms thread resolution works when a thread ID is present, but doesn't fully explore scenarios with null thread references. The implementation ap